          "type": "DiffNote",
          "body": "The comment is describing what happens in that code path currently, and all the layer numbers do get messed up. The #16109 workaround keeps TLS reassembly from being disturbed in that case, though there are some other issues, and removing it is a regression for #16109 unless MSP splitting is implemented.\n\nI believe that there's two ways it happens - the first is with out of order reassembly (applies to TCP only), and the second is when a subdissector returns DESEGMENT_ONE_MORE_SEGMENT so that the exact number of bytes needed isn't know, and then the next segment has the remaining bytes for the one PDU and the start of the next.\n\nWanting to deal with this issue is why I've left this in draft and started work on !6567 and !6666 and related issues, which is handling it at the TCP layer where it's more common - not handling it at the TCP layer can cause the same problems to occur in TLS in some cases as well, especially when there's more than one TCP layer in a single frame,  which can occur with various encapsulations (GSE, this 5G Lawful Intercept capture that someone posted, and caused me to create the other issue).",
